 1031 "Making Predictions Using Trend Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1033 3753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1034 0.962541274292324 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050210 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "369e4b42-eb06-50fe-866b-3c2859ec00eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050461 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050461 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050461 #edb/ref 1031 "Confidence Intervals for Linear Regression Intercept Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050461 #edb/ref 1033 4116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050461 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4b1a1cce-e35a-540f-9c78-90e0889cdb1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428733 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1031 "Trend Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1033 2609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050516 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "50060817-b302-5470-841b-1327afaae882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1031 "Linear Regression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1033 1057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "57cd3867-de9d-5ea0-b2c4-74cd8e11123f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094441 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094441 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094441 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186094441 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "67250215-1e03-538c-9563-cca87464372b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096594 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096594 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nConsider the data sets above. You are given that $\\overline{x}=4$ and $\\overline{y}=3.$ Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line. Round the slope and the $y$-intercept to $1$ decimal place if necessary." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096594 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15c6c8c2-9c56-5eff-bfc6-36cfe174ee3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096594 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny = mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m{:}$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(1-4)(6-3)+(2-4)(4-3) \\\\  & +(4-4)(2-3)+(9-4)(0-3) \\\\  & =(-3)(3)+(-2)(1)+(0)(-1)+(5)(-3) \\\\  & =-9-2-0-15 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(1-4)^{2}+(2-4)^{2}+(4-4)^{2}+(9-4)^{2} \\\\  & =9+4+0+25 \\\\  & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{-26}{38} = -\\dfrac{13}{19} \\approx -0.7.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =3-(-\\frac{13}{19})\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{109}{19} \\\\  & ≈5.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny = -0.7x + 5.7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096595 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096595 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Years ($x$)** | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Value ($y$)** | **$35$** | **$26$** | **$19$** | **$16$** |\n\nThe table above gives the value of a car a few years after it was purchased (in thousands of dollars). You are given that $\\overline{x}=2$ and $\\overline{y}=24.$ Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict how long it takes for the car's value to drop to $\\[math]10\\,000.$ Round your answer to the nearest year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096595 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c9247a2-0a43-5dec-b477-bb60f8f57c0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096595 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny = mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m{:}$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(0-2)(35-24)+(1-2)(26-24) \\\\  & +(3-2)(19-24)+(4-2)(16-24) \\\\  & =(-2)(11)+(-1)(2)+(1)(-5)+(2)(-8) \\\\  & =-22-2-5-16 \\\\  & =-45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm =-\\dfrac{45}{10} = -4.5.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =24-(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny = -4.5x+33\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate the required value by substituting $y=10$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.5x+33 \\\\ 10 & =-4.5x+33 \\\\ -23 & =-4.5x \\\\ x & ≈5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes approximately $5$ years for the car's value to drop to $\\[math]10\\,000.$\n\n**Note:** Since we're extrapolating (the point where $y=10$ is not within the original data range), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Regression Line Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096822 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0072fd14-c312-5229-9ffa-9b46c1ecd180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096823 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096823 #edb/ref 1039 "Making Predictions by Calculating a Regression Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096823 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096823 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f904b9b7-b565-5bee-8f6f-c319a7cb9d7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096859 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096859 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096859 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096859 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096859 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Regression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096859 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096859 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096859 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096859 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ef256051-a6fb-5783-ad98-e5e259f3af33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097078 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097078 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097078 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097078 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Age ($x$)** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Height ($y$)** | $95$ | $106$ | $110$ | $125$ |\n\nThe table above gives the ages, in years, and the corresponding heights, in centimeters, of Milly and Rasheed's four children. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=109.$ \n\nFind the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict the children's height when they are $6$ years old. Round the regression line's coefficients to three decimal places and your final answer to the nearest tenth of a centimeter." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8d3e7fb-7b7e-5708-a3c3-52c0d9f411c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097078 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097078 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(3-6)(95-109)+(5-6)(106-109) \\\\  & +(7-6)(110-109)+(9-6)(125-109) \\\\  & =(-3)(-14)+(-1)(-3)+(1)(1)+(3)(16) \\\\  & =42+3+1+48 \\\\  & =94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(3-6)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(7-6)^{2}+(9-6)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{94}{20}=\\frac{47}{10}=4.7.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =109-4.7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =109-28.2 \\\\  & =80.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=4.7x+80.8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can predict the height of a six-year-old child in this family by substituting $x=6$ into our regression line:\n\n$$\ny=4.7(6)+80.8=109\n$$\n\nTherefore, the model predicts that a six-year-old child will be $109.0\\,\\text{cm}$ tall." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097755 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097755 #edb/ref 1061 511836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097755 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77b46578-dd53-5487-a176-6e3dc8e5c0e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097755 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097756 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097756 #edb/ref 1061 511837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097756 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a58a1ac5-065b-5264-aac8-7c72c8376574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097756 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097757 #edb/ref 1061 511840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097757 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f467488-e1fc-585d-8dd6-b67aaa8f7740" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097757 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097892 #edb/ref 1064 "For a set of $n$ paired observations $x_i$ and $y_i,$ the trend line is the straight line that best fits the data.\n\n![](images/a2/a23a1411b40f723b19446d0838346dd74dd7bf75da918859cc688171e808b44a.png)\n\nFinding the equation of the trend line is called **linear regression**. The trend line is commonly called the **regression line** or **line of best fit.**\n\nWe can show that the equation of the trend line is given by\n\n$$\ny = mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- The slope $m$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $y$-intercept $b$ is given by\n\n$$\nb = \\overline{y} - m \\overline{x}.\n$$\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the means of the data sets:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} =  \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i, \\qquad \\overline{y} =  \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n y_i\n$$\n\n- The quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \n\\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nThere's some nice intuition behind the formula for $b.$ The trend line *always* passes through the point $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}).$ Therefore, this point must satisfy the trend line equation $y = mx+b.$ We can then solve this equation for $b.$\n\nAlso, notice that $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are the summation terms that feature in the formulas for the variance and covariance, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097892 #edb/ref 1065 5967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097892 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "13ca983a-da47-572a-911a-813833c034d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097892 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552871 #edb/ref 1080 "108.5\\,\\text{cm}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552871 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552871 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0336c168-4099-510b-a594-3b3934526e38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552882 #edb/ref 1080 "$78$ points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552882 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552882 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0cd4c0fb-caf1-5f4f-a432-bb5fd3ff247b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552923 #edb/ref 1080 "107.7\\,\\text{cm}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552923 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552923 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e747479-8878-5613-8c9c-88451ac3c939" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552930 #edb/ref 1080 "-x+9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552930 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552930 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "22c4dfcb-2d0e-5a9f-8507-74b8f3ae0fd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552941 #edb/ref 1080 "$7.2$ hours" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552941 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552941 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28a0a6b5-fa56-5177-8252-49d5bec965c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552978 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-1.6x+2" #edb/re