or $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =4-(-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =4+5 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-x+9.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553502 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553502 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553502 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $5$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nConsider the data sets above. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=2$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=3.$ Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line. Round the slope and the $y$-intercept to $1$ decimal place if necessary." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553502 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22d42a89-d817-5352-921e-6f3ec86ac62f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553502 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(0-2)(7-3)+(1-2)(5-3) \\\\  & +(3-2)(1-3)+(4-2)(-1-3) \\\\  & =(-2)(4)+(-1)(2)+(1)(-2)+(2)(-4) \\\\  & =-8-2-2-8 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-20}{10}=-2.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =3-(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3+4 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-2x+7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-316581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553522 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $9$ | $7$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nConsider the data sets above. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=4$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$ Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line. Round the slope and the $y$-intercept to **one decimal place** if necessary.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54d4d3b8-d729-5c07-8ab5-e817b0ad3339" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553522 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(1-4)(9-6)+(3-4)(7-6) \\\\  & +(5-4)(5-6)+(7-4)(3-6) \\\\  & =(-3)(3)+(-1)(1)+(1)(-1)+(3)(-3) \\\\  & =-9-1-1-9 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(1-4)^{2}+(3-4)^{2}+(5-4)^{2}+(7-4)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-20}{20}=-1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =6-(-1)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-x+10.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553546 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Years ($x$)** | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Value ($y$)** | **$20$** | **$18$** | **$15$** | **$11$** |\n\nThe table above gives the value of a motorcycle a few years after it was purchased (in thousands of dollars). You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=2$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=16.$ Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict how long it takes for the motorcycle to have a value of $$8\\,000.$ Round the answer to the nearest year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8625effc-a6bf-5c01-9019-062227a65c85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553546 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(0-2)(20-16)+(1-2)(18-16) \\\\  & +(3-2)(15-16)+(4-2)(11-16) \\\\  & =(-2)(4)+(-1)(2)+(1)(-1)+(2)(-5) \\\\  & =-8-2-1-10 \\\\  & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{21}{10}=-2.1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =16-(-2.1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-2.1x+20.2\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate the required value by substituting $y=8$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2.1x+20.2 \\\\ 8 & =-2.1x+20.2 \\\\ -12.2 & =-2.1x \\\\ x & ≈6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes $6$ years for the motorcycle to have a value of $$8\\,000.$\n\nNote: Since we're extrapolating (the point where $y=8$ is not within the original data range), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553551 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553551 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553551 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553551 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Weight $(kg)$ of Apples ($x$)** | **$30$** | **$44$** | **$50$** | **$52$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Hours ($y$)** | $5$ | $6$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n\nA farmer wants to understand the relation between the weight of apples to harvest and the time needed to do the job.\nThe table above gives the weight of apples harvested and the corresponding number of work hours over a sample of four days. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=44$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$ \n\nFind the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict the time needed when there are $20$ kilograms of apples to collect. Round the regression line's coefficients to three decimal places and your final answer to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553551 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92e8138f-b7f0-50e4-b13c-66a8af5c74ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553551 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(30-44)(5-6)+(44-44)(6-6) \\\\  & +(50-44)(6-6)+(52-44)(7-6) \\\\  & =(-14)(-1)+(0)(0)+(6)(0)+(8)(1) \\\\  & =14+0+0+8 \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(30-44)^{2}+(44-44)^{2}+(50-44)^{2}+(52-44)^{2} \\\\  & =196+0+36+64 \\\\  & =296\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{22}{296}=\\frac{11}{148}≈0.074.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{11}{148}\\cdot 44 \\\\  & ≈2.730\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=0.074x+2.730.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can predict the time needed to harvest $20$ kilograms by substituting $x=20$ into our regression line:\n\n$$\ny=0.074(20)+2.730≈4.2\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes $4.2$ hours to harvest $20\\,\\text{kg}$ of apples.\n\nNote: Since we're extrapolating (the point where $x=20$ is not within the original data range), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553568 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553568 #edb/ref 1069 "q-316324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553568 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553568 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $8$ | $6$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nConsider the data sets above. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=3$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=4.$ Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line. Round the slope and the $y$-intercept to **one decimal place** if necessary.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba58e3f6-198c-5bbf-9cbe-3fc18f85dcf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553568 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(1-3)(8-4)+(2-3)(6-4) \\\\  & +(4-3)(2-4)+(5-3)(0-4) \\\\  & =(-2)(4)+(-1)(2)+(1)(-2)+(2)(-4) \\\\  & =-8-2-2-8 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(1-3)^{2}+(2-3)^{2}+(4-3)^{2}+(5-3)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-20}{10}=-2.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =4-(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4+6 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-2x+10.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553572 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553572 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553572 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553572 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Study Hours ($x$)** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$1$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Average Grade ($y$)** | $71$ | $75$ | $65$ | $85$ |\n\nThe table above gives the number of study hours per day and the corresponding average grades (in points) of four students. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=2$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=74.$ \n\nFind the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict the average grade for a student that studies $3$ hours per day. Round the regression line's coefficients to three decimal places and your final answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553572 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc8754de-7209-53e4-a8f7-9e3f115b2f92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553572 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(1-2)(71-74)+(2-2)(75-74) \\\\  & +(1-2)(65-74)+(4-2)(85-74) \\\\  & =(-1)(-3)+(0)(1)+(-1)(-9)+(2)(11) \\\\  & =3+0+9+22 \\\\  & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0+1+4 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{34}{6}=\\frac{17}{3}≈5.667.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =74-\\frac{17}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{188}{3} \\\\  & ≈62.667\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=5.667x+62.667.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can predict the average grade of a student that studies $3$ hours per day by substituting $x=3$ into our regression line:\n\n$$\ny=5.667(3)+62.667≈80\n$$\n\nTherefore, the model predicts that a student that studies three hours per day will have an average grade of $80$ points." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553586 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553586 #edb/ref 1069 "q-365430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553586 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553586 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $13$ | $9$ | $5$ | $1$ |\n\nConsider the data sets above. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=7.$ Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line. Round the slope and the $y$-intercept to **one decimal place** if necessary.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553586 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ecc1a3cc-0f10-56ce-838a-ad42d1b426c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553586 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(3-6)(13-7)+(5-6)(9-7) \\\\  & +(7-6)(5-7)+(9-6)(1-7) \\\\  & =(-3)(6)+(-1)(2)+(1)(-2)+(3)(-6) \\\\  & =-18-2-2-18 \\\\  & =-40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(3-6)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(7-6)^{2}+(9-6)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-40}{20}=-2.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =7-(-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =7+12 \\\\  & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-2x+19.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553587 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553587 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553587 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553587 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Weeks ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$7$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Increase in Words per Minute ($y$)** | $20$ | $44$ | $102$ | $118$ |\n\nThe instructor of a $10$-week speed reading course monitors the students' progress during the course.\nThe table above gives the number of weeks of attendance and the corresponding increase in the number of words per minute for four students. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=71.$ \n\nFind the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict how many weeks are needed to see an increase of $50$ words per minute. Round the regression line's coefficients to three decimal places and your final answer to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553587 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0e8a005-d5ef-567f-838c-cadbb31dd290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553587 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(2-5)(20-71)+(3-5)(44-71) \\\\  & +(7-5)(102-71)+(8-5)(118-71) \\\\  & =(-3)(-51)+(-2)(-27)+(2)(31)+(3)(47) \\\\  & =153+54+62+141 \\\\  & =410\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(2-5)^{2}+(3-5)^{2}+(7-5)^{2}+(8-5)^{2} \\\\  & =9+4+4+9 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{410}{26}=\\frac{205}{13}≈15.769.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =71-\\frac{205}{13}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-7.846\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=15.769x-7.846\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate the required value by substituting $y=50$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =15.769x-7.846 \\\\ 50 & =15.769x-7.846 \\\\ 57.846 & =15.769x \\\\ x & ≈3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, students have an increase of $50$ words per minute after approximately $3.7$ weeks." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
